"""Year summaries, 1951-1975. See _year_summaries_1926_1950.py for notes on sourcing and accuracy."""
S = {
1951: "The Treasury-Federal Reserve Accord of March 1951 ended the wartime peg that held Treasury yields down, restoring the Fed's ability to set monetary policy independently of the government's financing needs. Inflation had jumped after the Korean War began, and the Fed, led by William McChesney Martin who became chairman that April, wanted to tighten without being forced to buy bonds to defend a fixed price. The accord laid the foundation for modern central banking and a market-determined bond market. Stocks rose through the year on strong earnings and defence spending, while price and wage controls were in force. The Korean War ground to a stalemate and armistice talks began in July. The accord is widely regarded as one of the key institutional events in US financial history.",
1952: "A strong equity year as the economy expanded on defence spending and consumer demand, and as the Korean War stalemate continued. Dwight Eisenhower, a popular general promising to end the war, won the presidential election in November, and investors welcomed his business-friendly outlook. Inflation had cooled after the 1951 spike and price controls were being dismantled. Steel strikes and a government seizure of the mills, struck down by the Supreme Court, were a notable labour episode. The Dow ended the year near 292, up about 8 percent, with dividend yields still well above bond yields, a valuation relationship that would reverse only in 1958. Television sales and the early suburban housing boom drove consumer industries.",
1953: "Stalin died in March and the Korean armistice was signed in July, easing geopolitical tension but also reducing defence demand. The Fed tightened earlier in the year, and the economy slid into a mild recession from mid-1953 into 1954. Stocks fell modestly, with the Dow losing about 4 percent, and the year ended near a low point of the correction. Treasury bond yields rose then fell as the downturn deepened. Eisenhower's administration pursued balanced budgets and cut taxes later. The recession was short, and by year end the monetary authorities had eased again, setting up a strong market advance in 1954.",
1954: "A year of strong gains. The mild recession ended by spring, and the stock market rose about 44 percent as earnings recovered and the Fed eased. On 23 November the Dow finally surpassed its September 1929 peak, 25 years after the crash, closing near 382, an event that restored public confidence in equities and drew a wave of retail investors back into the market. Congress held hearings on the 1929 crash, with Senator Fulbright's inquiry examining margin lending and speculation, and the Federal Reserve made clear it would watch margin credit. Interest rates were low and inflation negligible. The year marked the real beginning of the postwar equity culture.",
1955: "The bull market continued strongly, with the Dow rising about 20 percent, though the year included a sharp scare. On 26 September, after news of President Eisenhower's heart attack, the market suffered its biggest one-day drop since 1929, around 6.5 percent, before recovering within weeks as his health improved. The Fed started to raise rates to restrain a booming economy, with auto sales and housing at record levels. Inflation remained low. AFL and CIO unions merged in December, and the Montgomery bus boycott began, marking the start of the civil rights movement. The year showed how a shock to leadership can cause a sharp but brief panic when fundamentals remain strong.",
1956: "The economy expanded and stocks rose modestly while the Fed continued tightening to cool the boom. The Suez Crisis in October and November, when Britain, France and Israel attacked Egypt after Nasser nationalised the canal, briefly rattled oil markets and strained Anglo-American relations, and the Soviet crackdown in Hungary raised Cold War anxiety. President Eisenhower won reelection by a wide margin. Congress passed the Federal-Aid Highway Act, creating the Interstate Highway System, a vast public-works programme that spurred car, truck, construction and suburban development. The Dow closed the year slightly above 500 for the first time on a sustained basis in early 1956, passing a psychological milestone.",
1957: "A short, sharp recession began in the summer of 1957 and lasted into 1958, as tight credit, falling exports and an inventory correction hit output. The stock market declined about 12 percent in the year, with a low in October. On 4 October the Soviet Union launched Sputnik, the first artificial satellite, shocking the US and igniting the space race, which in turn boosted federal spending on science, education and defence. Americans questioned their technological lead, and the event prompted the creation of NASA the following year. The Fed began to cut rates late in the year as the downturn became clear. Asian flu spread and the Little Rock desegregation crisis made headlines.",
1958: "The economy rebounded strongly and equities soared, with the Dow gaining about 34 percent. A milestone occurred in this year: stock dividend yields fell below bond yields for the first time, reversing a relationship that had held since the 1920s and 1930s and reflecting investors' growing preference for growth over safety. Government spending on defence and the space race supported demand, and the Fed eased policy during the recession. The European Common Market launched in January, and the Eisenhower administration set up NASA. Inflation stayed low, around 1 to 2 percent, and the unemployment rate peaked near 7 percent in the recession before declining.",
1959: "A year of steady expansion and growth-stock enthusiasm, with the Dow up about 16 percent and electronics, chemicals and aerospace leading. A long steel strike from July through November disrupted output and later hit exports. The Fed tightened modestly to guard against inflation, and long-term Treasury yields rose to their highest in decades, though still around 4 percent. Fidel Castro took power in Cuba, introducing a new Cold War flashpoint close to the US. The 'glamour' stock phenomenon, in which investors paid high multiples for technology and science names, was building. Alaska and Hawaii became states, and the first commercial jet airliners entered service.",
1960: "The economy hit a mild recession from April 1960 to early 1961 as the Fed's tight policy and a decline in business investment took effect, and unemployment rose toward 7 percent. Stocks were roughly flat, ending with a small gain, after falling in the first half. John F. Kennedy narrowly defeated Richard Nixon in November, in part because voters blamed the recession on the Republicans. Balance-of-payments concerns emerged as gold began leaving the US, putting pressure on the Bretton Woods system and prompting the administration to take defensive measures. In the London gold market the price spiked in October above $40 an ounce, an early sign of strain on the $35 peg.",
1961: "A year of recovery in which stocks gained about 19 percent and 'glamour' electronics and technology companies attracted investor excitement. The Bay of Pigs invasion in April failed, and the Berlin Wall went up in August, raising Cold War tensions. Kennedy's tax proposals and expansionary stance helped lift demand, while the Fed pursued 'Operation Twist' in early 1961 to lower long rates while keeping short rates high to protect the dollar. Gold outflows continued. Economic growth resumed strongly after the recession, and inflation stayed below 2 percent. The year set up the market highs that would end abruptly in the first half of 1962.",
1962: "The 'Kennedy Slide' saw the market fall roughly 27 percent between December 1961 and a low on 26 June 1962, a drop triggered by President Kennedy's confrontation with US Steel over a price increase in April, overvalued glamour stocks and a general loss of confidence. It was the largest decline since 1946, and the SEC launched a Special Study of the Securities Markets afterwards. A recovery began in the second half, interrupted in October by the Cuban Missile Crisis, when the US and USSR came close to nuclear war, before the Soviets agreed to withdraw missiles. The market finished the year down about 11 percent. The crisis passed without a lasting hit to the economy.",
1963: "Stocks rebounded strongly, with the Dow rising about 17 percent, but the year ended in shock. On 22 November President Kennedy was assassinated in Dallas, and the NYSE closed early as prices dropped sharply, before reopening the following Tuesday, when the market rallied as Lyndon Johnson took office. Economic growth remained healthy and inflation low, around 1 percent. The tax cut that Kennedy had proposed was pending in Congress, and passed early in 1964 under Johnson. Martin Luther King delivered his 'I Have a Dream' speech in August, and the Fed raised the discount rate in July to defend the dollar.",
1964: "A strong expansion year, boosted by the Revenue Act cutting income and corporate taxes, which Johnson signed in February. Unemployment fell below 5 percent, growth topped 5 percent and corporate profits rose. The Dow gained about 15 percent. The Civil Rights Act was signed in July, and the Gulf of Tonkin incident in August led Congress to authorise a military escalation in Vietnam. Johnson won a landslide in November and launched his Great Society programmes. Inflation remained low, but the combination of tax cuts, rising social spending and a growing war would later push prices higher. The tax cut is often cited as a successful stimulus of a nearly full-employment economy.",
1965: "The 'go-go' investment era began, with fast-growing funds and performance-oriented managers chasing high-flying growth stocks. The economy ran near full employment, and Johnson committed ground troops to Vietnam, raising military spending without a matching tax increase. The Fed raised the discount rate in December, against the wishes of the White House, as inflation pressures built. The Dow rose about 10 percent and reached 969 in the year, nearing the 1,000 level for the first time. The Medicare and Medicaid programmes were enacted. Gold drained from the US as foreign central banks, led by France, demanded conversion of dollars, further straining the Bretton Woods system.",
1966: "A credit crunch. The Fed's tightening and a surge in demand for funds from corporations and the government caused interest rates to jump, and short-term yields rose above the ceilings set on deposits, draining money from savings institutions and housing. The Dow dropped about 22 percent from its February peak near 995 to an October low, and the market closed the year down 18 percent. Inflation rose above 3 percent as Vietnam spending expanded. The Fed eased in the autumn when growth slowed, and the market rebounded from its low. The episode showed how tight monetary policy and financial regulation could produce a sharp liquidity squeeze without a conventional recession.",
1967: "Stocks rebounded strongly, with the Dow gaining about 15 percent, led by speculative growth issues, conglomerates and performance funds. Conglomerates such as Litton and Ling-Temco-Vought used high-valued shares to acquire other companies, and mutual fund managers chased momentum. Inflation edged higher, and the Fed eased again earlier in the year, while the Vietnam War and Great Society spending pushed the budget deficit up. The Six-Day War in June and the closing of the Suez Canal affected oil markets, and race riots broke out in Detroit and Newark. Sterling was devalued in November, adding to pressure on the dollar and gold, whose price in London rose.",
1968: "A turbulent year. The Tet Offensive in January undermined US confidence in the Vietnam war, Martin Luther King and Robert Kennedy were assassinated, and urban unrest and the Democratic convention in Chicago stoked political crisis. Johnson declined to seek reelection, and Richard Nixon won in November. In March the London gold pool, through which central banks had defended the $35 price, was dissolved after heavy gold demand, creating a two-tier gold market. A tax surcharge was passed in June to curb inflation. The Dow peaked near 985 in December, just below the 1,000 mark, ahead of a long period of stagnation. The market was dominated by speculative go-go stocks.",
1969: "A bear market began as the Fed tightened sharply to fight inflation, which rose above 5 percent, and the Dow fell about 15 percent over the year, with the speculative issues hit hardest. Interest rates climbed to the highest levels in a century, with the prime rate reaching 8.5 percent. Apollo 11 landed astronauts on the Moon in July, a milestone for American confidence, and Nixon announced the policy of Vietnamisation. The economy peaked in December, leading into the 1970 recession. Conglomerate stocks and the go-go funds collapsed, ending a speculative era, and Wall Street's back-office paperwork crisis forced exchanges to close on Wednesdays.",
1970: "A recession ran through the year with unemployment rising above 6 percent, and the Fed eased policy. The stock market hit a bottom in late May after falling about 36 percent from its 1968 peak and then recovered, ending the year higher. The Penn Central Railroad filed for bankruptcy in June, the largest corporate failure to that date, and rattled the commercial paper market until the Fed provided liquidity. The Cambodia incursion and Kent State shootings stoked protest. Inflation remained stubborn near 6 percent, an early sign of stagflation, while Nixon began to consider wage and price controls. The Securities Investor Protection Corporation was created in response to brokerage failures.",
1971: "President Nixon ended the convertibility of the dollar into gold on 15 August, the 'Nixon shock', and imposed a 90-day wage and price freeze and a 10 percent import surcharge. This effectively ended the Bretton Woods system, as the Smithsonian Agreement in December tried and failed to patch it with revised parities. The dollar fell, gold began a bull run, and floating exchange rates would eventually become the norm. The Nasdaq launched on 8 February as the first electronic stock quotation system. Stocks rose modestly, ending the year up about 6 percent, helped by easy money and the price controls. The year reshaped the international monetary order and set the stage for the inflation of the 1970s.",
1972: "The market rose and on 14 November the Dow closed above 1,000 for the first time, ending the year near 1,020, a milestone after several failed attempts. The 'Nifty Fifty' era of large, high-quality growth stocks that investors would hold at any price took hold. Nixon won a landslide in November and visited China and Moscow, easing Cold War tension, and the US sold large quantities of grain to the Soviets, pushing up food prices. Easy money and the wage and price controls fuelled an election-year boom, while inflation lurked. Bretton Woods remained in disarray, with the dollar weak, and the Watergate break-in in June was the beginning of a scandal that would topple Nixon.",
1973: "The Arab-Israeli war in October led OPEC members to impose an oil embargo on the US and others, and crude oil prices quadrupled from about $3 to $12 a barrel by early 1974. The shock fed inflation, hit growth and ended the postwar expansion, with a recession beginning in November. The stock market, already weak because of tightening and Watergate, entered a bear market in January, and the Dow fell about 17 percent in the year. Currencies moved to floating rates in March. The Black-Scholes option pricing model was published and the Chicago Board Options Exchange opened, laying the foundation for modern derivatives. Price controls were gradually dismantled.",
1974: "One of the worst years for markets since the 1930s. President Nixon resigned in August following Watergate, and Gerald Ford took over. Inflation reached about 11 percent, oil prices stayed high, and the economy suffered a deep recession with unemployment rising toward 9 percent. The S&P 500 fell roughly 48 percent from its January 1973 peak to its low in late 1974, and the Dow dropped about 28 percent during the calendar year. The Franklin National Bank failure and the collapse of Herstatt in Germany exposed international banking risks. Stagflation, with simultaneous high inflation and unemployment, challenged Keynesian orthodoxy, and the Fed struggled to balance inflation and recession.",
1975: "A sharp market rebound followed the 1974 lows, with the S&P 500 gaining about 31 percent and the Dow around 38 percent, helped by easier money, a tax rebate and an economy emerging from recession. New York City nearly defaulted in the autumn, requiring state and federal rescue and prompting President Ford's famous 'Drop Dead' headline. On 1 May the SEC abolished fixed brokerage commissions, ending a century-old practice and ushering in discount brokerage and competitive trading. The Vietnam War ended with the fall of Saigon in April. Inflation eased to about 9 percent, but unemployment peaked above 8 percent. Equities were very cheap, which supported the rebound.",
}
